## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses distributed tracing fundamentals, cross-service context propagation, and practical validation strategies in CNPA stacks under load.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry tracing across HTTP, PostgreSQL, Kafka, and Redis
- Context propagation via HTTP headers (W3C Trace Context), Kafka headers, and Redis commands
- Span types: HTTP server, database query, Kafka publish/consume, and Redis operations
- Sampling strategies that balance overhead and visibility
- Observability dashboards and SLIs including p95/p99 latency, error rate, and consumer lag

## Code Example

```javascript
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { HttpInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
const { ExpressInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-express');
const { PgInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
const { KafkaInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-kafkajs');
const { RedisInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-ioredis');
const { JaegerExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-jaeger');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  serviceName: 'cnpa-service',
  instrumentations: [
    new HttpInstrumentation(),
    new ExpressInstrumentation(),
    new PgInstrumentation(),
    new KafkaInstrumentation(),
    new RedisInstrumentation(),
  ],
  traceExporter: new JaegerExporter(),
});

sdk.start();
```

This SDK setup instruments all components in the CNPA stack, automatically creating spans for HTTP requests, database queries, Kafka messages, and Redis operations with full context propagation.

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end visibility across services helps beginners diagnose where causality breaks in CNPA pipelines, a common production issue after deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, and messaging layers
- Trace propagation (W3C traceparent) and context maintenance
- Sampling strategies and exporters (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger/OTLP)
- End-to-end verification with test injections and trace reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example using OpenTelemetry in Node.js
const { trace } = require('@opentelemetry/api');
const tracer = trace.getTracer('cnpa-api');
async function handle(req, res){
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http_handler');
  // perform DB insert and Kafka publish here
  span.end();
  res.end('ok');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify trace propagation when a client library strips headers?
- How would you spot and fix sampling bias that hides rare failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical data correctness under retries in a CNPA stack.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent endpoints, Redis dedupe with TTL
- PostgreSQL upsert on a unique signup_id
- Kafka transactional writes and idempotent producer
- End-to-end verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: dedupe with Redis before DB write
if (redis.setnx('signup:'+signup_id, '1')) {
  redis.expire('signup:'+signup_id, 86400);
  // write to DB and publish
} else {
  // duplicate detected
  return 200;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor for undetected duplicates?
- What are failure modes if Redis goes down?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical consistency and latency guarantees across CNPA pipelines under load, focusing on real-world patterns beyond basic debugging.

## Key Concepts

- Transactional outbox across DB and Kafka
- Idempotent sinks and cache invalidation in Redis
- End-to-end correlation IDs and event timestamps
- Event-time processing and handling late events
- Canary rollouts and replay-based validation

## Code Example

```sql
-- Outbox pattern schema (example)
CREATE TABLE outbox (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_type TEXT NOT NULL,
  aggregate_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  payload JSONB NOT NULL,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  processed_at TIMESTAMPTZ
);
```

```javascript
// Pseudocode: propagate correlation and timestamp through HTTP and Kafka
function handleHttp(req) {
  const cid = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || generateCid();
  req.correlationId = cid;
  // attach to Kafka message headers
  publishToKafka({
    headers: { 'correlation_id': cid, 'ts': Date.now() },
    body: req.body
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test durability during partial outages and ensure no data loss?
- What metrics indicate freshness and how would you alert on drift?
